--  Asking about a file before acting on it.
--
--  This is the commonest conditional anybody writes in a shell, and until these
--  existed the language could not ask it at all: every other way a program
--  touched the filesystem went through running something, and running something
--  does not answer a question.
--
--  Three questions, because they are three different things. Something is
--  there; it is a directory; it is something this host could run. Acting on a
--  directory as though it were a file is the mistake the second one prevents.
--
--  A path nobody can reach -- no such directory, nothing named at all -- is not
--  a file, so the answer is False rather than a failure. A predicate that could
--  fail would need a second question beside every use, and the useful answer is
--  the same either way.

--  The working directory, which is somewhere by definition. A home directory
--  would have done as well on a machine that has one, and this file is checked
--  against a fixed output on machines that may not.
--
--  `Current_Directory` rather than `Output_Of ("pwd")`: where the session is
--  is something the shell knows, and asking a program for it means the example
--  only runs on hosts that ship that program.
Here : String := Current_Directory;

if Exists (Here) then
   put_line ("the working directory is there");
end if;

if Is_Directory (Here) then
   put_line ("and it is a directory");
end if;

if not Exists ("/adash-no-such-thing") then
   put_line ("and that is not there at all");
end if;

--  Which makes the shape a script uses before it does anything irreversible.
function Ready (Path : String) return Boolean is
begin
   return Exists (Path) and then not Is_Directory (Path);
end Ready;

if not Ready ("/adash-no-such-thing") then
   put_line ("nothing to work on");
end if;

--  Nothing here makes or unmakes a file, and nothing that answers a question
--  ever will: a shell that could delete one from an expression would make an
--  expression a thing with consequences. Writing is a command, where a reader
--  sees it happen -- see `writing.adash`. Removing is what programs are for,
--  and this shell runs programs.
